    <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition">
      <body>
        <div rend="parallel">
          <p xml:id="Aink430-kilavi">piriyum kol eṉṟu āṟṟāḷākiya talaimakaṭkut talaimakaṉ paruvavaravu kūṟi itu kāraṇattālum piriyēṉ eṉac colliyatu.</p>
          <p xml:lang="en">To the heroine who had become unable to endure thinking 'will [he] separate?', the hero declared the coming of the season and, so as to say, 'for this reason too, I will not leave', spoke [this].</p>
            </div>
        <div rend="parallel">
          <lg xml:id="Aink430">        
     <l>neṭumpoṟai micaiya kuṟuṅkāṟ koṉṟai</l>
        <l>yaṭarpoṉ ṉeṉṉac cuṭaritaḻ pakaruṅ</l>
        <l>kāṉkeḻu nāṭaṉ makaḷē</l>
        <l>yaḻuta lāṉṟici ṉaḻuṅkuval celavē.</l>       
        </lg>  
 <lg xml:lang="en">                    
<l>O daughter of the man from the land abounding in forests</l>
<l>where, atop the tall mountains, laburnum trees with short trunks</l>
<l>put forth<note place="foot" xml:lang="en"><foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">pakar-tal</foreign> has the standard meaning of 'to say, to utter, to announce' in the old corpus and a secondary meaning of 'to sell'. In the old commentary on this poem, it is glossed with <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">cōri-tal</foreign> ('to scatter'). Perhaps this is an extension of the base meaning 'to announce', in the sense that the laburnum tree is making public its petals.</note> glowing petals like gold plates,</l>
<l>please stop weeping. I will refrain from going.</l>  
          </lg>                      
        </div>
      </body>
    </text>
